chunk_findings.detail:
Both the stated intent ("end with a clear 'blocked on X, try later' exit code (2)") and the change's own new help text for the flag (`cli_help="never wait longer than this many seconds: exit 2 naming the live holder and how long it has held"`) promise that a wait which runs out its bound exits 2. The only code path that ends that way is still

    return O.nothing("wait", f"Still blocked after {round(waited)}s: ...", **data)

i.e. the exact same outcome object the pre-change `wait` already returned for a timed-out wait; the diff adds no exit-code handling anywhere (wait.py, surfaces/commands/lifecycle.py, surfaces/declared/lifecycle.py) and no test in the manifest asserts an exit status. Demonstrated input: `ddflow wait --max-s 5` on an item whose lease is renewed by another agent — the new code now stops after ~5 s and prints "…Held: <item> held by <holder> for Ns… Try later; `ddflow wait` again to keep waiting." but the process returns whatever `O.nothing` maps to, which is unchanged from the old unbounded wait, so a driver/script still cannot distinguish "item is mine now" from "I gave up" by exit status and will re-enter the same wait loop the change was meant to terminate. Either the blocked path must return an outcome that is documented/mapped to exit 2 (and be covered by a test), or the flag help and the stated contract must not advertise exit 2.

